# The journal is append-only (collectors/journal.py): a shard only ever grows at
# its end. When two collector runs append to the same shard, both runs' rows are
# wanted, so git keeps both sets instead of stopping on a conflict -- the rows
# already on the branch first, then the ones being rebased onto them. Restore
# passes over a row whose key is already loaded, first recorded wins, so a row
# that lands twice is never counted twice.
#
# The commit step in .github/workflows/collect.yml relies on this. Without it, two
# overlapping runs conflicted, and on 2026-09-25 run 323 lost an hour of rows that way.
state/**/*.jsonl merge=union
